WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4549.

2
00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.667
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.056
<v LILLIAN WASHINGTON>When I was born from . . .

4
00:00:03.056 --> 00:00:07.778
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Once . . . I'm going to intro and then we'll get started, because I got to get the date.

5
00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:09.444
So today is December second, 2015.

6
00:00:09.444 --> 00:00:11.111
I'm here with Ms. Lillian Washington.

7
00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:14.167
You're a hundred and two so . . . or a hundred and one.

8
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:14.444
<v WASHINGTON>One.

9
00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:15.833
<v ARSENEAULT>A hundred and one, okay.

10
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:16.944
A hundred and one.

11
00:00:16.944 --> 00:00:18.889
Going to be a hundred and two?

12
00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:20.000
<v WASHINGTON>January the sixth.

13
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:20.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Very soon.

14
00:00:20.615 --> 00:00:21.231
<v WASHINGTON>Nineteen six . . .

15
00:00:21.231 --> 00:00:22.769
<v ARSENEAULT>Nineteen . . . That was your birthday.

16
00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:24.923
She didn't grow up in Mossville, but

17
00:00:24.923 --> 00:00:28.923
<c.p>you lived in the Westlake [Louisiana] area and your kids went to Mossville.

18
00:00:28.923 --> 00:00:33.846
And so that's why we're interviewing you today, because you have some memories of the area.

19
00:00:33.846 --> 00:00:38.769
We just want to thank you so much for sharing your time with us today and . . .

20
00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:40.308
<v WASHINGTON>I'm glad to do it.

21
00:00:40.308 --> 00:00:40.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Awesome.

22
00:00:40.615 --> 00:00:42.462
<v WASHINGTON>If I can do it right.

23
00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:44.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh yeah, you're going to do great.

24
00:00:44.615 --> 00:00:48.000
So for the record can you state your full name?

25
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.250
<v WASHINGTON>Lillian Marie Comeaux Washington.

26
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.188
<v ARSENEAULT>And when and where were you born?

27
00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:58.688
<v WASHINGTON>In Carencro [Louisiana] right off on [Highway] 90.

28
00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:07.125
We had a big house on that and I was born there at the house.

29
00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:10.500
<v ARSENEAULT>You were born at the house.

30
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.312
Who helped . . . Who gave birth . . .?

31
00:01:13.312 --> 00:01:16.688
<v WASHINGTON>A mid-maid and doctor [Gelbow?], E.E.

32
00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:17.250
Gelbow.

33
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it common for children to be born in the home?

34
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.870
<v WASHINGTON>In the home.

35
00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:33.565
All born in the home. [Same?] doctor.

36
00:01:33.565 --> 00:01:42.174
<v ARSENEAULT>And your siblings . . . How many siblings did you have?

37
00:01:42.174 --> 00:01:45.043
<v WASHINGTON>We were thirteen.

38
00:01:45.043 --> 00:01:50.783
My oldest sister was named Sedalice.

39
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:55.565
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you spell it?

40
00:01:55.565 --> 00:01:56.522
<v WASHINGTON>S-E-D-A-L-I-C-E.

41
00:01:56.522 --> 00:02:02.261
And the next one, [Mary Lous?].

42
00:02:02.261 --> 00:02:04.174
M-A-R-Y L-O-U-S.

43
00:02:04.174 --> 00:02:09.913
And I was the third one.

44
00:02:09.913 --> 00:02:14.696
My name, Lillian Marie [Comeaux?].

45
00:02:14.696 --> 00:02:18.522
They all was [Comeaux?].

46
00:02:18.522 --> 00:02:22.348
Same daddy, same mama.

47
00:02:22.348 --> 00:02:28.087
Then you want the next one?

48
00:02:28.087 --> 00:02:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

49
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.447
<v WASHINGTON>Beatrice.

50
00:02:31.447 --> 00:02:37.237
She was Comeaux too.

51
00:02:37.237 --> 00:02:40.132
Then Julia.

52
00:02:40.132 --> 00:02:51.711
Then my pop had two brothers, [Palton?] Comeaux.

53
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:53.158
<v ARSENEAULT>Palton?

54
00:02:53.158 --> 00:02:57.500
<v WASHINGTON>Palton, P-A-L-T-O-N, Comeaux.

55
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:13.421
Then the next one was named like my daddy, Julian Comeaux.

56
00:03:13.421 --> 00:03:14.868
<v ARSENEAULT>Jullian?

57
00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:26.447
<v WASHINGTON>Then the next one was Edmo, E-D-M-O [Andy?].

58
00:03:26.447 --> 00:03:39.474
Then later on came another little girl, Julia Comeaux.

59
00:03:39.474 --> 00:03:40.921
<v ARSENEAULT>Julia?

60
00:03:40.921 --> 00:03:42.368
<v WASHINGTON>J-U-L-I-A.

61
00:03:42.368 --> 00:03:49.605
Then it start on brothers.

62
00:03:49.605 --> 00:03:52.500
Five brothers.

63
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.737
We had . . . Palton and Julian.

64
00:03:59.737 --> 00:04:05.526
Then they was born.

65
00:04:05.526 --> 00:04:15.658
Then the girls start coming . . . Lula May.

66
00:04:15.658 --> 00:04:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Lula May?

67
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.839
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] How many does that make?

68
00:04:24.839 --> 00:04:28.871
<v ARSENEAULT>I should have been counting.

69
00:04:28.871 --> 00:04:30.484
I wasn't.

70
00:04:30.484 --> 00:04:30.684
Sorry.

71
00:04:30.484 --> 00:04:31.290
<v WASHINGTON>[ . . . ?]

72
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.903
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Seven boys.

73
00:04:32.903 --> 00:04:35.323
<v WASHINGTON>Then Lula May.

74
00:04:35.323 --> 00:04:40.161
The two last ones was [Odelia?].

75
00:04:40.161 --> 00:04:41.774
That's one.

76
00:04:41.774 --> 00:04:45.000
She died very young.

77
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.419
Then Lula May.

78
00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:53.065
And the last boy was John Roosevelt.

79
00:04:53.065 --> 00:04:54.677
<v ARSENEAULT>John Roosevelt?

80
00:04:54.677 --> 00:04:57.903
He was the baby?

81
00:04:57.903 --> 00:05:03.548
<v WASHINGTON>[Died very young?] She had the measles.

82
00:05:03.548 --> 00:05:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old was she when she died?

83
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.472
<v WASHINGTON>Maybe three or four months.

84
00:05:13.472 --> 00:05:16.250
He was very young [ . . .?].

85
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.333
Had the measles.

86
00:05:18.333 --> 00:05:24.583
And I believe I give you all there are.

87
00:05:24.583 --> 00:05:30.833
I'd conjure them see if I gave you all.

88
00:05:30.833 --> 00:05:32.917
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Barbara and Mary.

89
00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:37.083
<v WASHINGTON>I thought I said Mary Rue.

90
00:05:37.083 --> 00:05:38.472
<v ARSENEAULT>Mary Rue?

91
00:05:38.472 --> 00:05:41.944
That's a lot of siblings.

92
00:05:41.944 --> 00:05:46.806
<v WASHINGTON>But she was from the first group.

93
00:05:46.806 --> 00:05:48.194
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

94
00:05:48.194 --> 00:05:56.528
<v WASHINGTON>See that leaves Mary Louise and Lillian, then Julia . . . then starts Palton.

95
00:05:56.528 --> 00:05:57.917
<v ARSENEAULT>That's five.

96
00:05:57.917 --> 00:05:58.611
<v WASHINGTON>Julius.

97
00:05:58.611 --> 00:06:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Six.

98
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.259
<v WASHINGTON>Edma.

99
00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:02.519
<v ARSENEAULT>Seven.

100
00:06:02.519 --> 00:06:13.852
<v WASHINGTON>Then came [Beatrice?], and Lula May and John Roosevelt.

101
00:06:13.852 --> 00:06:16.370
<v ARSENEAULT>That's ten.

102
00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.889
<v WASHINGTON>That's ten.

103
00:06:18.889 --> 00:06:21.407
Three more.

104
00:06:21.407 --> 00:06:23.926
<v ARSENEAULT>Then you.

105
00:06:23.926 --> 00:06:27.704
<v WASHINGTON>And Mary Rue.

106
00:06:27.704 --> 00:06:28.963
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Barbara.

107
00:06:28.963 --> 00:06:34.000
<v WASHINGTON>And Barbara, B-A-R-B-A-R-A.

108
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, and then . . .

109
00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:36.778
<v WASHINGTON>Then Lillian.

110
00:06:36.778 --> 00:06:38.444
<v ARSENEAULT>And then you.

111
00:06:38.444 --> 00:06:40.667
You were the third . . .

112
00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:41.778
<v WASHINGTON>And Julia.

113
00:06:41.778 --> 00:06:43.444
You got her.

114
00:06:43.444 --> 00:06:45.111
<v ARSENEAULT>That's thirteen, yeah.

115
00:06:45.111 --> 00:06:46.778
There you go.

116
00:06:46.778 --> 00:06:49.000
<v WASHINGTON>[ . . .?] There was that.

117
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.071
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you describe your house growing up?

118
00:06:52.071 --> 00:06:54.704
<v WASHINGTON>The house was a big house.

119
00:06:54.704 --> 00:06:59.969
Something my dad built it for somebody wealthy and we lived there.

120
00:06:59.969 --> 00:07:03.041
We had more rooms than we needed.

121
00:07:03.041 --> 00:07:05.673
<v ARSENEAULT>So someone let y'all live there?

122
00:07:05.673 --> 00:07:07.867
Or your dad built it?

123
00:07:07.867 --> 00:07:11.378
<v WASHINGTON>No, it was there when we got there.

124
00:07:11.378 --> 00:07:11.816
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

125
00:07:11.816 --> 00:07:22.786
<v WASHINGTON>And we had all kind of fruit trees in the yard: fig, all kind of different fig, pear, plum, everything we needed was right there.

126
00:07:22.786 --> 00:07:27.173
We was raised in a nice place, and we stayed

127
00:07:27.173 --> 00:07:30.245
<c.p>right there until we all grow up.

128
00:07:30.245 --> 00:07:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many rooms?

129
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.289
<v WASHINGTON>And we had cow.

130
00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:36.005
We had pig.

131
00:07:36.005 --> 00:07:37.721
We had dog.

132
00:07:37.721 --> 00:07:39.438
We had everything.

133
00:07:39.438 --> 00:07:40.582
Everything right.

134
00:07:40.582 --> 00:07:54.313
And when I started working, they made you work, my mom and them, and a lot of them raised chicken, geese, duck, turkey, everything.

135
00:07:54.313 --> 00:07:56.601
And we had cow.

136
00:07:56.601 --> 00:07:58.318
They had horse.

137
00:07:58.318 --> 00:07:59.462
Had mule.

138
00:07:59.462 --> 00:08:09.188
And I was the one to take the place of a boy to work with my daddy.

139
00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:14.910
I'd go get the . . . around the horses from the pasture.

140
00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:20.059
Bring them home and feed them in the morning.

141
00:08:20.059 --> 00:08:28.641
And I remember good when my cousin, my daddy hired him to work with us.

142
00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:33.218
And he told me, "Go feed them animals.”

143
00:08:33.218 --> 00:08:40.083
And I told him, “I’m not going to go feed them animals.”

144
00:08:40.083 --> 00:08:44.660
You the . . . My daddy paying you to work."

145
00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.665
And he went and got the [buggy?].

146
00:08:48.665 --> 00:08:53.242
You don't know what a [buggy?] whip is.

147
00:08:53.242 --> 00:08:54.958
The worst one.

148
00:08:54.958 --> 00:08:58.391
He whipped me with the [buggy?].

149
00:08:58.391 --> 00:09:01.824
I cried for, oh Lord, months.

150
00:09:01.824 --> 00:09:05.829
Every time I see him I cry.

151
00:09:05.829 --> 00:09:09.262
I’d say, "My daddy pay you.

152
00:09:09.262 --> 00:09:12.122
Go feed your own horse."

153
00:09:12.122 --> 00:09:16.127
But I had to go get her.

154
00:09:16.127 --> 00:09:28.714
I had a horse that stayed close to the yard, and in the morning when four o'clock I get on the horse.

155
00:09:28.714 --> 00:09:40.156
I go riding the other horse and bring him by the house . . . bring them by the barn and feed them.

156
00:09:40.156 --> 00:09:43.017
The feed was right there.

157
00:09:43.017 --> 00:09:45.878
Yes, and I cried days.

158
00:09:45.878 --> 00:09:52.743
And that's the only whipping I had in my life after that.

159
00:09:52.743 --> 00:09:55.604
That was a big one.

160
00:09:55.604 --> 00:09:58.465
And I milked the cow.

161
00:09:58.465 --> 00:10:03.614
I milked two cows every morning and every afternoon.

162
00:10:03.614 --> 00:10:06.474
We had our own milk.

163
00:10:06.474 --> 00:10:08.763
Got our own butter.

164
00:10:08.763 --> 00:10:10.479
We had everything.

165
00:10:10.479 --> 00:10:21.350
Then when you went to work in the field we had to work pick cotton when it's cotton time.

166
00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.066
He plant corn.

167
00:10:23.066 --> 00:10:32.220
When they had corn good enough to eat, our own corn, cut it and cook it.

168
00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:41.946
We'd plant some sweet potato, Irish potato, even popcorn when it was time to plant the corn.

169
00:10:41.946 --> 00:10:47.667
We plant our own popcorn and our own corn, make

170
00:10:47.667 --> 00:10:49.956
<c.p>our own corn meal.

171
00:10:49.956 --> 00:11:01.399
They had a place not too far from Mossville that grind the corn, make it some corn meal, flour, whatever.

172
00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:03.687
We had it there.

173
00:11:03.687 --> 00:11:11.125
And when it come to work in the field you’d plant the corn,

174
00:11:11.125 --> 00:11:19.707
you’d plant sugarcane, plant cotton when the season for cotton, corn, Irish potatoes, sweet potatoes.

175
00:11:19.707 --> 00:11:21.995
<v ARSENEAULT>You said Irish potatoes?

176
00:11:21.995 --> 00:11:26.000
What kind of potato is that?

177
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.780
<v WASHINGTON>Irish potato is . . . Irish potato, white potato.

178
00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:30.320
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

179
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.480
<v WASHINGTON>To make salads with.

180
00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:34.640
You never seen that?

181
00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.420
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn't know it was called that.

182
00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.120
I just call them potatoes.

183
00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.820
<v WASHINGTON>You just call them potatoes.

184
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.900
Irish potato.

185
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:48.680
And we had our own corn meal.

186
00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.380
We had our own milk.

187
00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.080
We had our own butter.

188
00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.700
We plant sugarcane.

189
00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:58.400
We have our own sugarcane.

190
00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.640
<v ARSENEAULT>How would you make the sugar?

191
00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:11.360
<v WASHINGTON>They had a place that grind the corn, and the sugarcane is just . . . You saw the sugarcane plant.

192
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.680
That's all we had to buy was sugar.

193
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's all you got at the store?

194
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:26.788
<v WASHINGTON>We had . . . We raised potato, sweet potato, Irish potato, and we didn't raise rice.

195
00:12:26.788 --> 00:12:29.212
We had to buy that.

196
00:12:29.212 --> 00:12:32.606
And sugar, we had to buy that.

197
00:12:32.606 --> 00:12:36.485
But my mama raised a lot of chicken.

198
00:12:36.485 --> 00:12:40.848
If she needed something we'd go to the store.

199
00:12:40.848 --> 00:12:42.788
We’d sell her chicken.

200
00:12:42.788 --> 00:12:46.667
Trade it in for some . . . whatever we needed.

201
00:12:46.667 --> 00:12:50.061
But we raised everything you could eat.

202
00:12:50.061 --> 00:12:54.909
And when I tell them they say, "What y'all ate?"

203
00:12:54.909 --> 00:12:57.818
I say, "Fresh food every day."

204
00:12:57.818 --> 00:13:01.697
Say, "What you mean it was fresh food?"

205
00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:07.515
I say, "Well we had corn when it’s time for the corn.

206
00:13:07.515 --> 00:13:08.970
We'd break it.

207
00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:12.848
We’d make some maque choux, they called it.

208
00:13:12.848 --> 00:13:14.788
Corn on the cob."

209
00:13:14.788 --> 00:13:16.727
<v ARSENEAULT>It's called maque choux?

210
00:13:16.727 --> 00:13:18.182
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] In French.

211
00:13:18.182 --> 00:13:19.636
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

212
00:13:19.636 --> 00:13:20.606
<v WASHINGTON>It's corn.

213
00:13:20.606 --> 00:13:21.576
<v ARSENEAULT>Maque choux.

214
00:13:21.576 --> 00:13:22.545
<v WASHINGTON>Maque choux.

215
00:13:22.545 --> 00:13:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Maque choux.

216
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:24.492
<v WASHINGTON>Yes.

217
00:13:24.492 --> 00:13:26.951
We called them sweet potato.

218
00:13:26.951 --> 00:13:29.410
We had that from year-round.

219
00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:38.262
We had a little store house we put the sweet potato, Irish potato, all different one in there.

220
00:13:38.262 --> 00:13:41.213
And my daddy made some syrup.

221
00:13:41.213 --> 00:13:43.672
We had our own syrup.

222
00:13:43.672 --> 00:13:47.115
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they make it from the cane?

223
00:13:47.115 --> 00:13:47.607
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

224
00:13:47.607 --> 00:13:51.049
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember how he made it?

225
00:13:51.049 --> 00:13:51.541
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

226
00:13:51.541 --> 00:13:56.459
They had a machine that they could ground up corn.

227
00:13:56.459 --> 00:13:58.918
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, it was corn syrup.

228
00:13:58.918 --> 00:13:59.410
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

229
00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:07.279
Juice and they make they call the syrup of the juice and it turn to syrup.

230
00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:09.246
We had our own.

231
00:14:09.246 --> 00:14:13.180
So we didn't buy nothing much, you know.

232
00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.639
Didn't have to charge nothing.

233
00:14:15.639 --> 00:14:19.082
We raised everything we needed at home.

234
00:14:19.082 --> 00:14:19.574
Yeah.

235
00:14:19.574 --> 00:14:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would your mom cook?

236
00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.335
<v WASHINGTON>My mama she would just cook.

237
00:14:26.335 --> 00:14:29.449
She wouldn't work in the field like us.

238
00:14:29.449 --> 00:14:32.562
We had to go work in the field.

239
00:14:32.562 --> 00:14:35.286
But my mama would cook for us.

240
00:14:35.286 --> 00:14:43.849
Around eight o'clock she'd come meet up in the field with our breakfast if we didn't want to go home and eat.

241
00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:44.627
We’d eat

242
00:14:44.627 --> 00:14:46.573
<c.p>just out in the field.

243
00:14:46.573 --> 00:14:49.297
<v ARSENEAULT>She'd bring your breakfast in the field?

244
00:14:49.297 --> 00:14:49.686
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

245
00:14:49.686 --> 00:14:53.968
<v ARSENEAULT>What time did you have to get up in the morning?

246
00:14:53.968 --> 00:15:01.751
<v WASHINGTON>We get up around four sharp, because I have to go round up the cows . . . the horses at four o'clock.

247
00:15:01.751 --> 00:15:04.086
But we was used to it.

248
00:15:04.086 --> 00:15:05.254
It didn't matter.

249
00:15:05.254 --> 00:15:07.200
And we'd take a break.

250
00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.535
She’d make us take a break.

251
00:15:09.535 --> 00:15:11.092
She’d come with breakfast.

252
00:15:11.092 --> 00:15:14.984
If we wanted to come home and eat we could.

253
00:15:14.984 --> 00:15:16.151
My mama cooked.

254
00:15:16.151 --> 00:15:18.486
She cooked lunch at dinner time.

255
00:15:18.486 --> 00:15:20.043
And she cooked anything.

256
00:15:20.043 --> 00:15:24.324
She asked if we wanted what kind of meat we want.

257
00:15:24.324 --> 00:15:25.492
She had chicken.

258
00:15:25.492 --> 00:15:27.827
She'd kill one and cook it.

259
00:15:27.827 --> 00:15:30.162
<v ARSENEAULT>Had everything you needed right there.

260
00:15:30.162 --> 00:15:30.551
Wow.

261
00:15:30.551 --> 00:15:30.941
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

262
00:15:30.941 --> 00:15:36.000
And they have a little store close off where we would trade.

263
00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:38.059
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the store?

264
00:15:38.059 --> 00:15:40.632
<v WASHINGTON>Right on the Highway 90.

265
00:15:40.632 --> 00:15:45.265
You know when you go where that school is?

266
00:15:45.265 --> 00:15:48.353
You know where Arceneaux Street is?

267
00:15:48.353 --> 00:15:52.985
<v ARSENEAULT>[Disagrees] I'm not really . . . Is this in Carencro [Louisiana]?

268
00:15:52.985 --> 00:15:57.618
<v WASHINGTON>That'd be before you get to . . . Right in town.

269
00:15:57.618 --> 00:16:00.706
But they say passed that now . . .

270
00:16:00.706 --> 00:16:01.221
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

271
00:16:01.221 --> 00:16:02.250
<v WASHINGTON>. . . is Carencro.

272
00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:09.971
But it used to be just a street on [Highway] 90 going to Opelousas [Louisiana].

273
00:16:09.971 --> 00:16:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

274
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.927
<v WASHINGTON>And they had a store.

275
00:16:13.927 --> 00:16:19.780
It was a small little man, a little French man.

276
00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:21.537
He'd take chicken.

277
00:16:21.537 --> 00:16:28.561
He'll trade anything you have with what you needed in the store.

278
00:16:28.561 --> 00:16:32.659
We would mostly buy sugar and coffee.

279
00:16:32.659 --> 00:16:37.927
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh yeah, because I guess coffee doesn't grow here.

280
00:16:37.927 --> 00:16:40.854
Can't really grow coffee here.

281
00:16:40.854 --> 00:16:45.537
Do you remember the name of the store?

282
00:16:45.537 --> 00:16:49.634
<v WASHINGTON>[Sye?] Jones they used to call it.

283
00:16:49.634 --> 00:16:50.805
Sye Jones.

284
00:16:50.805 --> 00:16:53.146
That's all I know.

285
00:16:53.146 --> 00:16:57.244
But he was very friendly little man.

286
00:16:57.244 --> 00:16:59.585
He had a family.

287
00:16:59.585 --> 00:17:04.268
His wife had something wrong with her leg.

288
00:17:04.268 --> 00:17:06.610
She was nice too.

289
00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:10.122
She taught us Catechism at church.

290
00:17:10.122 --> 00:17:11.293
Very nice.

291
00:17:11.293 --> 00:17:15.390
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the name of the church?

292
00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:18.902
<v WASHINGTON>Saint Peter and Paul in Carencro.

293
00:17:18.902 --> 00:17:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it close to your home?

294
00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:27.118
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] But we had a buggy and we had horses.

295
00:17:27.118 --> 00:17:31.647
You could see the buggy on the picture on the wall.

296
00:17:31.647 --> 00:17:32.882
<v ARSENEAULT>I see it.

297
00:17:32.882 --> 00:17:35.353
Did y'all all fit in there?

298
00:17:35.353 --> 00:17:36.176
All thirteen?

299
00:17:36.176 --> 00:17:38.235
<v WASHINGTON>No. We'd go different times.

300
00:17:38.235 --> 00:17:39.882
They had different masses.

301
00:17:39.882 --> 00:17:40.706
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

302
00:17:40.706 --> 00:17:43.176
<v WASHINGTON>Sometimes we would walk to church.

303
00:17:43.176 --> 00:17:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

304
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:49.855
<v WASHINGTON>But we would ride as many as we could in there.

305
00:17:49.855 --> 00:17:53.048
It wasn't far from the house.

306
00:17:53.048 --> 00:17:57.306
And we enjoyed our life in the children.

307
00:17:57.306 --> 00:18:05.823
We had a place my mama made the girls play in the corner of the yard.

308
00:18:05.823 --> 00:18:17.532
We used to have fun with dolls and then take the backyard my brother and they played marbles and we played jack.

309
00:18:17.532 --> 00:18:20.194
We played with dolls, too.

310
00:18:20.194 --> 00:18:21.258
We'd make

311
00:18:21.258 --> 00:18:23.919
<c.p>some dolls with some rags.

312
00:18:23.919 --> 00:18:27.113
And my daddy would watch us.

313
00:18:27.113 --> 00:18:29.774
When the corn had some . . .

314
00:18:29.774 --> 00:18:30.839
<v ARSENEAULT>The tassels.

315
00:18:30.839 --> 00:18:32.968
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, had the hair.

316
00:18:32.968 --> 00:18:40.419
We say . . . We’d steal it from my daddy and put in on our dolls.

317
00:18:40.419 --> 00:18:40.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Hair.

318
00:18:40.952 --> 00:18:46.806
<v WASHINGTON>If he would catch us he'd fuss at us for that.

319
00:18:46.806 --> 00:18:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What else would you play?

320
00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:51.367
<v WASHINGTON>We played jack.

321
00:18:51.367 --> 00:18:52.735
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

322
00:18:52.735 --> 00:18:54.102
<v WASHINGTON>Jacks, you know.

323
00:18:54.102 --> 00:18:56.381
They got some over here.

324
00:18:56.381 --> 00:19:01.851
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it the little . . . And you drop the ball and pick them . . .

325
00:19:01.851 --> 00:19:03.219
<v WASHINGTON>We played that.

326
00:19:03.219 --> 00:19:05.953
We played all kind of game.

327
00:19:05.953 --> 00:19:09.600
And on weekends growing up we'd play ball.

328
00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:15.070
And we have some white neighbors called the [Seraronos?], they would come

329
00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.349
<c.p>play ball with us too.

330
00:19:17.349 --> 00:19:18.260
<v ARSENEAULT>Like baseball?

331
00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:28.288
<v WASHINGTON>And our cousin [ . . .?] Arceneaux, he would make ice cream on weekends on Saturday and Sunday and sell it a nickel a cone.

332
00:19:28.288 --> 00:19:32.391
While we're playing ball the children would get up.

333
00:19:32.391 --> 00:19:37.860
They had the big pasture in the front, like this right here.

334
00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:39.228
Bigger than it.

335
00:19:39.228 --> 00:19:42.419
We'd play ball, girl and boy together.

336
00:19:42.419 --> 00:19:45.153
And that's how . . . and watch us.

337
00:19:45.153 --> 00:19:47.888
Or parents would come watch us.

338
00:19:47.888 --> 00:19:51.079
And my cousins would sell ice cream.

339
00:19:51.079 --> 00:19:55.637
We'd stop and eat ice cream, a nickel a cone.

340
00:19:55.637 --> 00:19:59.740
We had a good time when we was young.

341
00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:03.842
And some people say, "Oh, it was slave town."

342
00:20:03.842 --> 00:20:05.665
I can't say that.

343
00:20:05.665 --> 00:20:09.312
And when I got old enough I know.

344
00:20:09.312 --> 00:20:10.679
I was tall.

345
00:20:10.679 --> 00:20:12.958
At seven I was tall.

346
00:20:12.958 --> 00:20:19.795
And the [Beche’s?] wanted me to go stay with the children when they was somewhere.

347
00:20:19.795 --> 00:20:25.265
They come pick me up and I go stay with the kids.

348
00:20:25.265 --> 00:20:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you working for them?

349
00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:28.393
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

350
00:20:28.393 --> 00:20:28.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

351
00:20:28.786 --> 00:20:33.500
<v WASHINGTON>We was on the farm that the land belonged to the [Beche’s?]

352
00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:34.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

353
00:20:34.286 --> 00:20:35.857
<v WASHINGTON>We just leased it.

354
00:20:35.857 --> 00:20:37.821
<v ARSENEAULT>So y'all farmed for them?

355
00:20:37.821 --> 00:20:38.214
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

356
00:20:38.214 --> 00:20:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

357
00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:42.745
<v WASHINGTON>And I raised two of them boys.

358
00:20:42.745 --> 00:20:45.955
<v ARSENEAULT>You helped raise the [Basahay?] children?

359
00:20:45.955 --> 00:20:46.490
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

360
00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:47.025
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

361
00:20:47.025 --> 00:20:50.236
<v WASHINGTON>The grandchildren . . . it was her grandchildren.

362
00:20:50.236 --> 00:20:53.981
There are two boys: Trey and Burt.

363
00:20:53.981 --> 00:20:54.516
<v ARSENEAULT>Burt?

364
00:20:54.516 --> 00:21:00.936
<v WASHINGTON>And Burt they say he was a priest, but I don't know.

365
00:21:00.936 --> 00:21:03.611
I took care of them.

366
00:21:03.611 --> 00:21:07.357
And then I worked for the Conque.

367
00:21:07.357 --> 00:21:11.102
They was some neighbors who were close.

368
00:21:11.102 --> 00:21:13.242
I worked for the

369
00:21:13.242 --> 00:21:13.777
<c.p>Conque.

370
00:21:13.777 --> 00:21:25.548
And she wanted to go and vacation, her and her husband, and they wanted someone but they brought us in Algier’s at [?].

371
00:21:25.548 --> 00:21:34.108
And while we was there, the old lady say, "Y'all must spend two week with me.

372
00:21:34.108 --> 00:21:38.389
But I'm going to take y'alls visit [Algier?].

373
00:21:38.389 --> 00:21:42.134
Different place they have, I'll bring y'all."

374
00:21:42.134 --> 00:21:45.344
She brought us to church first.

375
00:21:45.344 --> 00:21:46.949
Went to church.

376
00:21:46.949 --> 00:21:49.624
It was a white church.

377
00:21:49.624 --> 00:21:52.834
Blacks . . . But I sit with them.

378
00:21:52.834 --> 00:22:01.395
And the next day she took us to Maison Blanche and they had the rolling step . . .

379
00:22:01.395 --> 00:22:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The escalator.

380
00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:07.153
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] And she treat y'all for ice cream.

381
00:22:07.153 --> 00:22:10.787
She wanted to treat us ice cream.

382
00:22:10.787 --> 00:22:13.902
And she said, "Whatever y'all want."

383
00:22:13.902 --> 00:22:16.498
So they say, "Ice cream."

384
00:22:16.498 --> 00:22:23.247
And at that time I didn't know about the black people couldn't go.

385
00:22:23.247 --> 00:22:23.767
<v ARSENEAULT>Segregation.

386
00:22:23.767 --> 00:22:26.362
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] Well it wasn't me.

387
00:22:26.362 --> 00:22:29.477
Allan, he ordered the ice cream.

388
00:22:29.477 --> 00:22:38.822
And so when it came to sit down and eat, the clerk say, "She can't sit with y'all."

389
00:22:38.822 --> 00:22:43.495
And he say, "Grandma, Lil can't sit with us?"

390
00:22:43.495 --> 00:22:45.571
And she said, "No?"

391
00:22:45.571 --> 00:22:49.725
He said, "Well what you want to do?"

392
00:22:49.725 --> 00:22:56.993
She said, "I don't want my ice cream if Lil can't sit with us.

393
00:22:56.993 --> 00:23:00.627
She be with us all the time."

394
00:23:00.627 --> 00:23:04.780
And said, "Norbert, you want your ice cream?"

395
00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:11.530
He said, "No. If Lil can't sit, no. No, I don't want mine."

396
00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:14.125
And, "How about you Lil?"

397
00:23:14.125 --> 00:23:17.760
I say, "I can't sit here anyhow."

398
00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:21.913
But I didn't think it was for real.

399
00:23:21.913 --> 00:23:23.990
And really we didn't.

400
00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:25.028
We left.

401
00:23:25.028 --> 00:23:28.143
We didn't eat the ice cream.

402
00:23:28.143 --> 00:23:31.258
She said, asked the lady again.

403
00:23:31.258 --> 00:23:33.334
And she, yes again.

404
00:23:33.334 --> 00:23:34.892
She said, "Why?"

405
00:23:34.892 --> 00:23:39.045
But the lady wouldn't tell her . . . tell him.

406
00:23:39.045 --> 00:23:43.199
She say, "She just cannot sit with y'all."

407
00:23:43.199 --> 00:23:52.024
And the grandma said, "Well y'all don't want the ice cream, give it back to the lady."

408
00:23:52.024 --> 00:23:53.582
And we left.

409
00:23:53.582 --> 00:24:02.408
<c.p>And you know, it never bothered me concerning that until I got here over a year ago[?].

410
00:24:02.408 --> 00:24:10.195
I went to the movie in Lake Charles [Louisiana] in Westlake . . . I say Lake Charles.

411
00:24:10.195 --> 00:24:11.233
That's it.

412
00:24:11.233 --> 00:24:17.463
And they showed the movie and Oprah played the part [about us?].

413
00:24:17.463 --> 00:24:21.098
And it brought it back to me.

414
00:24:21.098 --> 00:24:23.174
I said, "Oh Lord."

415
00:24:23.174 --> 00:24:25.251
<v ARSENEAULT>What movie was that?

416
00:24:25.251 --> 00:24:29.404
<v WASHINGTON>I didn't know I was mistreated until now.

417
00:24:29.404 --> 00:24:30.962
<v ARSENEAULT>Selma [movie name].

418
00:24:30.962 --> 00:24:32.000
Yeah.

419
00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:38.859
<v WASHINGTON>And we went back and everywhere else they didn’t do nothing like that.

420
00:24:38.859 --> 00:24:42.025
But they sure did with me.

421
00:24:42.025 --> 00:24:50.466
So I say, "Oh yeah, I was like Martin Luther King." [laughs] And didn't know it.

422
00:24:50.466 --> 00:24:51.521
That's right.

423
00:24:51.521 --> 00:24:55.215
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about his assassination?

424
00:24:55.215 --> 00:24:56.270
<v WASHINGTON>That's all.

425
00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:58.380
That's all I remember.

426
00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:00.491
I never was mistreated.

427
00:25:00.491 --> 00:25:03.656
The children would sleep with me.

428
00:25:03.656 --> 00:25:07.877
They didn't want to go with their parents.

429
00:25:07.877 --> 00:25:11.043
They thought I was their mama.

430
00:25:11.043 --> 00:25:17.374
I don't know what they . . . I asked somebody when I went home.

431
00:25:17.374 --> 00:25:22.123
I said, "Y'all know what Norbert and Allan at?"

432
00:25:22.123 --> 00:25:31.092
They said, "No." I said, "They must be somewhat grownup big men, or maybe they left town.

433
00:25:31.092 --> 00:25:32.147
You know?"

434
00:25:32.147 --> 00:25:38.479
There were some called Conque, C-O-N-Q-U-E, and the daddy was George Conque.

435
00:25:38.479 --> 00:25:39.006
<v ARSENEAULT>George?

436
00:25:39.006 --> 00:25:39.534
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

437
00:25:39.534 --> 00:25:40.061
<v ARSENEAULT>Conque.

438
00:25:40.061 --> 00:25:43.227
And that was the [Beshae's?] grandchildren?

439
00:25:43.227 --> 00:25:43.755
No?

440
00:25:43.755 --> 00:25:47.448
<v WASHINGTON>That was some neighbors across from us.

441
00:25:47.448 --> 00:25:48.503
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

442
00:25:48.503 --> 00:25:49.031
Okay.

443
00:25:49.031 --> 00:25:52.724
<v WASHINGTON>The grandchildren, there was a little girl.

444
00:25:52.724 --> 00:25:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were they when you were raising them?

445
00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.871
<v WASHINGTON>They were about seven . . . six and seven.

446
00:26:01.871 --> 00:26:10.166
All five of the seven were little boys, and I stayed there until I married.

447
00:26:10.166 --> 00:26:12.377
I was growing up.

448
00:26:12.377 --> 00:26:15.695
I still was working for them.

449
00:26:15.695 --> 00:26:16.248
Yeah.

450
00:26:16.248 --> 00:26:18.460
Now they old men.

451
00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:21.225
If they're still alive maybe.

452
00:26:21.225 --> 00:26:23.990
I'm lucky to be alive.

453
00:26:23.990 --> 00:26:25.649
I don't know.

454
00:26:25.649 --> 00:26:29.520
But they was nice people to me.

455
00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:30.073
Yeah.

456
00:26:30.073 --> 00:26:35.050
And when I grew up I didn't have boyfriend.

457
00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:37.815
I didn't want no boyfriend.

458
00:26:37.815 --> 00:26:43.344
I would dance with them, but I didn’t . . . that's it.

459
00:26:43.344 --> 00:26:53.851
And one dance and make it to [ . . .?] Arceneaux house and invited whoever they want to come to the dance.

460
00:26:53.851 --> 00:26:56.616
And I saw my husband.

461
00:26:56.616 --> 00:27:01.593
There was three or four boy at the door.

462
00:27:01.593 --> 00:27:08.228
And I had two good friend, girls, that were neighbors with us.

463
00:27:08.228 --> 00:27:19.841
They told me they both did, but I said, "Oh I saw nice boys at the door and a black boy,”

464
00:27:19.841 --> 00:27:26.477
like I was white. [laughs] And they say, "How come you pick

465
00:27:26.477 --> 00:27:28.136
<c.p>the blackest one?"

466
00:27:28.136 --> 00:27:35.325
I said, "Well, if I dance with him he will be my husband."

467
00:27:35.325 --> 00:27:38.089
They say, "Shut your mouth."

468
00:27:38.089 --> 00:27:40.301
They curse me out.

469
00:27:40.301 --> 00:27:43.619
And it's real, I'm not lying.

470
00:27:43.619 --> 00:27:46.384
I'm telling y'all the truth.

471
00:27:46.384 --> 00:27:57.997
I had never talked to a boy to talk with a boy, but if I go to a dance I dance.

472
00:27:57.997 --> 00:28:09.609
Only time I speak with the . . . And the first one I talk with my daddy didn't like the family, or whatever.

473
00:28:09.609 --> 00:28:14.586
And he wanted to whip me for that boy.

474
00:28:14.586 --> 00:28:19.010
But he didn't visit me at my house.

475
00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:33.387
But then, when I see around there and it really happened that the other boys . . . some white boy would come to the dance with us too.

476
00:28:33.387 --> 00:28:36.705
But them boys was light complexion.

477
00:28:36.705 --> 00:28:41.682
And they were some [Melancon?] and some [Mouton?] too.

478
00:28:41.682 --> 00:28:45.000
And them girls was [Hebert?].

479
00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:45.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Hebert?

480
00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:48.500
<v WASHINGTON>[ . . .?] and they said, "Shut your mouth.

481
00:28:48.500 --> 00:28:50.000
Shut you mouth.

482
00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.000
You ain't going to."

483
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:57.000
And then he danced with them two other girls first.

484
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:05.500
Then he danced with me and I said, "Now I got it." [laughs] [ . . .?] And it really happened.

485
00:29:05.500 --> 00:29:08.500
And I didn't know the boy.

486
00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:10.500
First I'd seen him.

487
00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:18.500
I know that was the first time he see me too, but he didn't tell me.

488
00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:26.500
And he came to see me the Sunday . . . that was Saturday night and he came up.

489
00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:33.000
And my dad, when he came home, my daddy said, "Who's that boy?"

490
00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.000
“I don't know him.

491
00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:37.000
I didn't know him.

492
00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:40.000
I just saw him, you know.”

493
00:29:40.000 --> 00:29:44.000
And he say he didn't come to stay.

494
00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:45.500
Told my mom.

495
00:29:45.500 --> 00:29:49.500
He said, "Ma'am, I didn't come to stay.

496
00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:52.000
I just want her address."

497
00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.500
I give him the address.

498
00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:58.000
And he said, "I'll write to you.

499
00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:01.500
I'm going to Alexandria [Louisiana] to work."

500
00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:06.500
And he wrote to me, and I answered him back.

501
00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:12.000
And he said he would come to Saint Paul on Sunday.

502
00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:16.500
And he did come, and he asked my dad.

503
00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:20.000
He had asked me on the letter.

504
00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:22.000
"Would you marry me?"

505
00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:24.500
And I answer him yes.

506
00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.500
I didn't know him.

507
00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:29.000
So he came that Sunday.

508
00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.500
My dad's sister

509
00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:38.000
<c.p>was sick and she was in the bed, and I was laying down by her.

510
00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:40.500
Somebody knock on the door.

511
00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.500
I said, "Come in."

512
00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:44.500
And that was him.

513
00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:48.500
And my mama and daddy both was there.

514
00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.500
They was sitting outside.

515
00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:53.500
My daddy said, "Who are you?"

516
00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:56.000
He had a loud voice.

517
00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:05.500
And he said his name, and he look at me because I was there but I didn’t say nothing.

518
00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:07.000
I was ashamed.

519
00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:10.500
And he asked them to get married.

520
00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.500
And we got married.

521
00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How old were you?

522
00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:17.706
<v WASHINGTON>I was old enough.

523
00:31:17.706 --> 00:31:27.853
I was old enough to be married because my mama said, "Oh, she thinks she's [?].

524
00:31:27.853 --> 00:31:35.971
She old enough if she want to be married because she old.

525
00:31:35.971 --> 00:31:42.059
She just made her birthday and she was twenty-one."

526
00:31:42.059 --> 00:31:49.500
Three week after that we was married at the Catholic church.

527
00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:56.265
And we stayed married until he got sick and died.

528
00:31:56.265 --> 00:31:56.941
1991.

529
00:31:56.941 --> 00:31:59.647
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his name?

530
00:31:59.647 --> 00:32:00.324
<v WASHINGTON>Adam.

531
00:32:00.324 --> 00:32:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Adam.

532
00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:02.353
<v WASHINGTON>Adam Washington.

533
00:32:02.353 --> 00:32:15.882
Then we went to work at the dairy farm on the highway going to Scott [Louisiana] . . . Scott to Lafayette [Louisiana].

534
00:32:15.882 --> 00:32:19.941
And we stayed there two years.

535
00:32:19.941 --> 00:32:30.088
Then, later when we decided to come here, but I had my first little child.

536
00:32:30.088 --> 00:32:33.471
We work in the farm.

537
00:32:33.471 --> 00:32:40.912
I didn't go in the filed because I had little children.

538
00:32:40.912 --> 00:32:46.324
And I had my second one, Mary Lou.

539
00:32:46.324 --> 00:32:49.029
<c.p>And she right there.

540
00:32:49.029 --> 00:32:53.765
And we moved here in Westlake [Louisiana].

541
00:32:53.765 --> 00:32:57.824
I had two kids . . . two girls.

542
00:32:57.824 --> 00:33:10.000
I went to work for a lady who had asthma, and I could bring my children with me.

543
00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:12.706
And they were little.

544
00:33:12.706 --> 00:33:22.176
But that's not the old lady name, but she was staying with her daughter.

545
00:33:22.176 --> 00:33:24.206
She had asthma.

546
00:33:24.206 --> 00:33:27.588
She was good to me.

547
00:33:27.588 --> 00:33:33.000
And I could take my children, and Gerald.

548
00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:36.382
His place was right there.

549
00:33:36.382 --> 00:33:49.912
He grew up to be a big boy, but when they were little I put them in the same bed.

550
00:33:49.912 --> 00:33:57.353
And watched them and took care of the other old lady.

551
00:33:57.353 --> 00:34:07.500
When they was [good enough to go back to school?], then I worked different place.

552
00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:21.029
I went to wash for white people at night and iron their clothes so I could do something for money.

553
00:34:21.029 --> 00:34:24.412
And I'm still in Westlake.

554
00:34:24.412 --> 00:34:34.559
I had my two girls, and I had two boys. [Joe?] was my oldest one.

555
00:34:34.559 --> 00:34:37.265
His picture’s up there.

556
00:34:37.265 --> 00:34:41.324
And Gerald was my third one.

557
00:34:41.324 --> 00:34:46.059
Then I had [Sherrill?] and Mary Louise.

558
00:34:46.059 --> 00:34:50.794
Mary Lou is . . . Mary Lou right there.

559
00:34:50.794 --> 00:34:59.588
Then on the last I had two daughters again, Sherrill and then Cathy.

560
00:34:59.588 --> 00:35:02.294
Cathy was the baby.

561
00:35:02.294 --> 00:35:05.000
<v ARSENEUALT>So four total?

562
00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:06.955
<v WASHINGTON>I had four girls.

563
00:35:06.955 --> 00:35:09.398
<v ARSENEAULT>Four girls and two boys?

564
00:35:09.398 --> 00:35:10.376
<v WASHINGTON>Two boys.

565
00:35:10.376 --> 00:35:14.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, so two girls, two boys, two girls.

566
00:35:14.286 --> 00:35:15.752
Okay, so six.

567
00:35:15.752 --> 00:35:19.662
<v WASHINGTON>And they went to school, and I worked.

568
00:35:19.662 --> 00:35:23.571
I worked for a good lady, and she

569
00:35:23.571 --> 00:35:24.549
<c.p>wasn't married.

570
00:35:24.549 --> 00:35:26.992
She lived in Lake Charles.

571
00:35:26.992 --> 00:35:29.436
She had a nice home.

572
00:35:29.436 --> 00:35:31.391
She go to work.

573
00:35:31.391 --> 00:35:39.211
I worked for her on weekends, and I work at the cafeteria at [ . . .?] during the week.

574
00:35:39.211 --> 00:35:42.632
I was a waitress in the front.

575
00:35:42.632 --> 00:35:46.053
I did that [till they grow up?].

576
00:35:46.053 --> 00:35:51.917
I wanted Mary Louise to go to college, because she had asthma.

577
00:35:51.917 --> 00:35:59.248
From she was six years old when she caught that asthma, and I had the [ . . .?].

578
00:35:59.248 --> 00:36:05.602
She graduated, well she went to McNeese [State University in Lake Charles, Louisiana].

579
00:36:05.602 --> 00:36:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did all your children go to Mossville High?

580
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:19.333
<v WASHINGTON>They went to Mossville from the beginning, and from there they went to McNeese.

581
00:36:19.333 --> 00:36:23.333
She said they gave her trouble.

582
00:36:23.333 --> 00:36:24.667
<v ARSENEAULT>At McNeese?

583
00:36:24.667 --> 00:36:31.333
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] About the first black going to school down there.

584
00:36:31.333 --> 00:36:36.000
Then Mary Lou went to McNeese too.

585
00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:40.667
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you first hear about Mossville?

586
00:36:40.667 --> 00:36:53.333
<v WASHINGTON>When the first years . . . I don't remember when the years what started going to school . . . I don’t remember that.

587
00:36:53.333 --> 00:36:59.333
But Mary Louise and Mary Lou went to Mossville.

588
00:36:59.333 --> 00:37:00.000
Sharon [ . . .?].

589
00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.333
<v UNIDENTIFIED WOMAN>It was in the ‘50s when we went [ . . . ?].

590
00:37:05.333 --> 00:37:06.667
<v ARSENEAULT>To Mossville?

591
00:37:06.667 --> 00:37:15.333
<v WASHINGTON>And then when they integrate the school, Sharon and them went to . . . Mossville.

592
00:37:15.333 --> 00:37:16.667
<v UNIDENTIFIED WOMAN>Westlake High.

593
00:37:16.667 --> 00:37:20.667
<v ARSENEAULT>When they . . . What year was that?

594
00:37:20.667 --> 00:37:21.333
Remember?

595
00:37:21.333 --> 00:37:26.000
Like '59 I think . . . or '69 rather.

596
00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:32.000
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>I went to Mossville from 1959 to '62.

597
00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:34.733
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, they got that picture.

598
00:37:34.733 --> 00:37:36.373
They all graduate.

599
00:37:36.373 --> 00:37:39.653
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Mossville?

600
00:37:39.653 --> 00:37:46.213
Did you ever . . . What was like your first impression of the area?

601
00:37:46.213 --> 00:37:46.760
<v WASHINGTON>Where?

602
00:37:46.760 --> 00:37:47.853
<v ARSENEAULT>Of Mossville.

603
00:37:47.853 --> 00:37:50.587
<v WASHINGTON>It was nice to me.

604
00:37:50.587 --> 00:37:52.227
It was alright.

605
00:37:52.227 --> 00:37:59.333
But I visit them for little PTA or when they . . . for the school.

606
00:37:59.333 --> 00:38:02.067
And my children played ball.

607
00:38:02.067 --> 00:38:05.893
My oldest son liked to play football,

608
00:38:05.893 --> 00:38:06.987
<c.p>baseball too.

609
00:38:06.987 --> 00:38:08.080
<v ARSENEAULT>For Mossville?

610
00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:08.627
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

611
00:38:08.627 --> 00:38:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any of the coaches?

612
00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:14.159
<v WASHINGTON>Coach Williams.

613
00:38:14.159 --> 00:38:17.635
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about him?

614
00:38:17.635 --> 00:38:22.270
<v WASHINGTON>He was nice, but I . . . He was nice.

615
00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:24.008
They were nice.

616
00:38:24.008 --> 00:38:26.905
I can't complain about them.

617
00:38:26.905 --> 00:38:29.222
I never had trouble.

618
00:38:29.222 --> 00:38:33.857
My children all have been in no trouble.

619
00:38:33.857 --> 00:38:39.071
They were boy scouts when they was old enough.

620
00:38:39.071 --> 00:38:42.548
I put them in boy scouts.

621
00:38:42.548 --> 00:38:48.921
And then none of them didn’t go to jail . . . [but?] one.

622
00:38:48.921 --> 00:38:51.238
But that's [ . . .?] big trouble.

623
00:38:51.238 --> 00:39:02.246
But not one of them, they have they had to call me and tell me go see about them.

624
00:39:02.246 --> 00:39:03.405
You know?

625
00:39:03.405 --> 00:39:06.302
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>You followed the football team.

626
00:39:06.302 --> 00:39:07.460
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, yeah.

627
00:39:07.460 --> 00:39:09.198
And play ball.

628
00:39:09.198 --> 00:39:10.357
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>In Mossville.

629
00:39:10.357 --> 00:39:13.254
<v WASHINGTON>They had a good record.

630
00:39:13.254 --> 00:39:16.730
My oldest son make me laugh.

631
00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:21.944
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh you were in the . . . Is this the band?

632
00:39:21.944 --> 00:39:26.000
What do you remember about this?

633
00:39:26.000 --> 00:39:27.612
<v WASHINGTON>She did good.

634
00:39:27.612 --> 00:39:32.449
She fell and [broke herself off?] on a bicycle.

635
00:39:32.449 --> 00:39:34.061
<v ARSENEAULT>Wait, what happened?

636
00:39:34.061 --> 00:39:36.748
<v WASHINGTON>She fell riding a bicycle.

637
00:39:36.748 --> 00:39:39.972
She broke her back or something.

638
00:39:39.972 --> 00:39:43.196
I don't know what is was.

639
00:39:43.196 --> 00:39:45.346
But she was good.

640
00:39:45.346 --> 00:39:49.107
They didn't give me no . . . any trouble.

641
00:39:49.107 --> 00:39:51.257
I can't say nothing.

642
00:39:51.257 --> 00:39:55.556
Like the one that's sick, he killed himself.

643
00:39:55.556 --> 00:39:58.780
Gerald was a good little boy.

644
00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:04.692
Not because he was mine, but that was a good child.

645
00:40:04.692 --> 00:40:12.215
And Joe was good too, but he made me laugh just his sweetness funny.

646
00:40:12.215 --> 00:40:25.112
He said when they pick him up for the service and they has a bunch of boys, you know, and they pick him up.

647
00:40:25.112 --> 00:40:31.023
And they was calling his name and he had a nickname [. . .?].

648
00:40:31.023 --> 00:40:31.561
<v ARSENEAULT>What?

649
00:40:31.561 --> 00:40:32.636
<v WASHINGTON>[Douga Boy?].

650
00:40:32.636 --> 00:40:34.248
I don't know.

651
00:40:34.248 --> 00:40:38.009
That was his little nickname, [Douga?] Boy.

652
00:40:38.009 --> 00:40:42.846
He said the captain was calling Joseph Adam Washington.

653
00:40:42.846 --> 00:40:49.832
Said for the longest, and he not going to get up and say,

654
00:40:49.832 --> 00:40:57.355
you know, the man came by him and he say, "Boy what's your name?"

655
00:40:57.355 --> 00:40:59.505
He said, "[Douga?] Boy."

656
00:40:59.505 --> 00:41:02.192
I said, "[Douga?] Boy what?"

657
00:41:02.192 --> 00:41:03.804
He said, "Washington."

658
00:41:03.804 --> 00:41:09.178
And he said, "Boy, you don't know your real name?"

659
00:41:09.178 --> 00:41:14.014
"No," he said he didn't know his real name.

660
00:41:14.014 --> 00:41:15.089
Just knew

661
00:41:15.089 --> 00:41:16.164
<c.p>[Douga?] Boy.

662
00:41:16.164 --> 00:41:18.313
<v ARSENEAULT>He knew his nickname.

663
00:41:18.313 --> 00:41:21.000
<v WASHINGTON>And he [right there?].

664
00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:26.971
<v ARSENEAULT>I was going to ask you . . . where were your parents from?

665
00:41:26.971 --> 00:41:35.114
<v WASHINGTON>In . . . At least my grandpa, on my daddy's side, they was living in Lafayette [Louisiana].

666
00:41:35.114 --> 00:41:41.629
<v ARSENEAULT>Your grandfather on your dad . . . Your dad's father was living in Lafayette?

667
00:41:41.629 --> 00:41:44.343
Do you remember his name?

668
00:41:44.343 --> 00:41:48.686
<v WASHINGTON>Pierre Comeaux, and his wife was Maria Comeaux.

669
00:41:48.686 --> 00:41:53.029
But she was an Arceneaux before she married.

670
00:41:53.029 --> 00:41:56.286
And they grow up down there.

671
00:41:56.286 --> 00:42:00.086
They worked in Lafayette; they stayed there.

672
00:42:00.086 --> 00:42:02.800
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they do there?

673
00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:07.143
<v WASHINGTON>Before, I don't know where they come from.

674
00:42:07.143 --> 00:42:13.657
They say they had come on a boat wherever they was from.

675
00:42:13.657 --> 00:42:16.371
<v ARSENEAULT>They came on a boat?

676
00:42:16.371 --> 00:42:18.000
<v WASHINGTON>On a boat.

677
00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:27.771
And they say when somebody would get sick on the boat they would drop it in the river.

678
00:42:27.771 --> 00:42:32.114
I don't know how true that was but . . .

679
00:42:32.114 --> 00:42:35.371
<v ARSENEAULT>They would throw the person over-board?

680
00:42:35.371 --> 00:42:37.000
Oh my!

681
00:42:37.000 --> 00:42:41.444
<v WASHINGTON>But I don't know where they was from.

682
00:42:41.444 --> 00:42:48.111
They never say, and neither on my mama's side . . . my grandmother's side.

683
00:42:48.111 --> 00:42:54.222
But I know they were Arceneaux, my grandmother on daddy's side.

684
00:42:54.222 --> 00:42:58.111
And my mama's side they were Matthews.

685
00:42:58.111 --> 00:42:58.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Matthews?

686
00:42:58.667 --> 00:43:00.333
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] And [Senegal?].

687
00:43:00.333 --> 00:43:03.111
<v ARSENEAULT>[Senegal?] Do you remember them?

688
00:43:03.111 --> 00:43:07.000
Did you grow up around your grandparents?

689
00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:10.333
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, not too far from them.

690
00:43:10.333 --> 00:43:14.778
And then went . . . They was staying in Carencro.

691
00:43:14.778 --> 00:43:25.333
They moved from Carencro to Kinder [Louisiana], and on my mama's side that's where they were staying; in Kinder.

692
00:43:25.333 --> 00:43:29.222
You know where Kinder is around here?

693
00:43:29.222 --> 00:43:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I've seen the signs for it, but I've never been there.

694
00:43:37.000 --> 00:43:44.212
<v WASHINGTON>And my daddy, on his side they was from Lafayette.

695
00:43:44.212 --> 00:43:49.981
After that they moved to where we went.

696
00:43:49.981 --> 00:43:55.750
He got married very young with my mama.

697
00:43:55.750 --> 00:43:57.913
She was young.

698
00:43:57.913 --> 00:44:00.798
Her mama had died.

699
00:44:00.798 --> 00:44:04.404
And my aunts raise him.

700
00:44:04.404 --> 00:44:12.337
I remember my aunt that raised them, she raised us too.

701
00:44:12.337 --> 00:44:15.221
They was [Senegal?] too.

702
00:44:15.221 --> 00:44:18.106
<v ARSENEAULT>They lived in Carencro?

703
00:44:18.106 --> 00:44:24.596
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] They lived close to Mouton [Switch?] in Carencro.

704
00:44:24.596 --> 00:44:27.481
That's . . . I remember that.

705
00:44:27.481 --> 00:44:44.067
And when one of us was sick, well she would . . . the old lady was there to help my mama take care of us.

706
00:44:44.067 --> 00:44:46.952
And she was old.

707
00:44:46.952 --> 00:44:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she have any home remedies?

708
00:44:52.000 --> 00:45:00.959
<v WASHINGTON>And then I went to school when they make a school for the black kids and the Indian children.

709
00:45:00.959 --> 00:45:04.260
So that's when I went to school.

710
00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:07.089
I was in the eighth grade.

711
00:45:07.089 --> 00:45:09.919
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>I don’t think she heard you.

712
00:45:09.919 --> 00:45:13.691
The remedies, I don’t think she heard you.

713
00:45:13.691 --> 00:45:16.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all have any natural remedies?

714
00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:20.764
Did your aunt use any natural remedies, home remedies?

715
00:45:20.764 --> 00:45:21.707
<v WASHINGTON>Home what?

716
00:45:21.707 --> 00:45:23.122
<v ARSENEAULT>Like home medicine?

717
00:45:23.122 --> 00:45:25.951
<v WASHINGTON>Oh yeah, that's all we did.

718
00:45:25.951 --> 00:45:28.780
We didn't know nothing about medicine.

719
00:45:28.780 --> 00:45:35.854
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of stuff would she do, like if you got a cold or something?

720
00:45:35.854 --> 00:45:37.740
<v WASHINGTON>[ . . .?] castor oil for sure.

721
00:45:37.740 --> 00:45:43.398
So when you start coughing they'd clean your body with some oil.

722
00:45:43.398 --> 00:45:45.756
They called it castor oil.

723
00:45:45.756 --> 00:45:48.585
I never went to the doctor.

724
00:45:48.585 --> 00:45:49.057
<v ARSENEAULT>Never?

725
00:45:49.057 --> 00:45:50.000
Wow.

726
00:45:50.000 --> 00:45:51.596
<v WASHINGTON>Never was sick.

727
00:45:51.596 --> 00:45:54.789
<v ARSENEAULT>What about any kind of herbs?

728
00:45:54.789 --> 00:46:04.367
<v WASHINGTON>And in all the diseases that went around like chicken pox, the mumps, well I caught the mumps.

729
00:46:04.367 --> 00:46:08.092
And I didn't catch no chicken pox.

730
00:46:08.092 --> 00:46:10.752
I didn't catch no measles.

731
00:46:10.752 --> 00:46:13.945
That’s what my mama told me.

732
00:46:13.945 --> 00:46:21.394
I was the only one that didn't catch all the disease that went around.

733
00:46:21.394 --> 00:46:33.633
I got one little brother, John, that last one, he died with the . . . whatever they call when children catch the like a measle.

734
00:46:33.633 --> 00:46:35.761
He died with that.

735
00:46:35.761 --> 00:46:37.358
He had pneumonia.

736
00:46:37.358 --> 00:46:40.018
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Didn't Pop make some teas?

737
00:46:40.018 --> 00:46:41.615
Some herbal teas?

738
00:46:41.615 --> 00:46:45.339
<v WASHINGTON>Well he would treat people, you know.

739
00:46:45.339 --> 00:46:46.936
I don't know.

740
00:46:46.936 --> 00:46:48.000
With something.

741
00:46:48.000 --> 00:46:55.450
Later on . . . I remember it real well because I would go bring her medicine.

742
00:46:55.450 --> 00:47:07.156
He would go in the woods, and he . . . I should know how to do it because I boiled the tea for him.

743
00:47:07.156 --> 00:47:10.349
He had a big black pot.

744
00:47:10.349 --> 00:47:13.541
He would go in the woods.

745
00:47:13.541 --> 00:47:18.862
I'd go with him, and he would get different things.

746
00:47:18.862 --> 00:47:29.505
Some plants, they are flowers in the yard . . . some by the yard, and they make like a fan, the leaf.

747
00:47:29.505 --> 00:47:33.761
I remember him putting that in the water.

748
00:47:33.761 --> 00:47:39.615
And he sure would heal people, and they would get well.

749
00:47:39.615 --> 00:47:42.807
But I don't remember the rest.

750
00:47:42.807 --> 00:47:46.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was a healer?

751
00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:50.841
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] And he would let some whiskey too.

752
00:47:50.841 --> 00:47:53.866
And he would sell it.

753
00:47:53.866 --> 00:47:55.682
<v ARSENEAULT>He made whiskey?

754
00:47:55.682 --> 00:47:58.102
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, with some corn.

755
00:47:58.102 --> 00:47:59.917
I'd help him.

756
00:47:59.917 --> 00:48:12.019
Anything he had to do he'd always make me help him because I was the tallest one . . . the oldest one.

757
00:48:12.019 --> 00:48:25.331
My oldest sister, she was smarter I say because she always complaining sick that they wouldn't make her do nothing that much.

758
00:48:25.331 --> 00:48:27.146
Thought she sick.

759
00:48:27.146 --> 00:48:30.172
Seems like it's not real.

760
00:48:30.172 --> 00:48:33.803
And I never taste the whiskey.

761
00:48:33.803 --> 00:48:40.459
I work with my daddy and I never taste the whiskey.

762
00:48:40.459 --> 00:48:48.930
My sister next to me, she would drink when my daddy didn't see her.

763
00:48:48.930 --> 00:48:51.955
One day she got drunk.

764
00:48:51.955 --> 00:48:53.166
Real little.

765
00:48:53.166 --> 00:48:58.611
When she got better my daddy would whip her.

766
00:48:58.611 --> 00:49:01.637
But to taste that whiskey.

767
00:49:01.637 --> 00:49:05.268
And you would sell the liquor.

768
00:49:05.268 --> 00:49:07.688
He made [beer?] too.

769
00:49:07.688 --> 00:49:12.529
Make tea for the one that was sick.

770
00:49:12.529 --> 00:49:19.790
<c.p>Yeah, and when he got sick he died by himself, you know.

771
00:49:19.790 --> 00:49:22.210
We all was married.

772
00:49:22.210 --> 00:49:24.025
All was gone.

773
00:49:24.025 --> 00:49:36.732
But one sister was staying with him, and he must have caught a stroke or a heart attack because the doctors,

774
00:49:36.732 --> 00:49:47.624
when they find him in his house he was so decayed they could smell him from way far.

775
00:49:47.624 --> 00:49:56.701
It's a neighbor passed by the road and he saw the house porch was light.

776
00:49:56.701 --> 00:49:59.121
Still light in daytime.

777
00:49:59.121 --> 00:50:05.172
Said, "Oh no this man never keeps his light on.

778
00:50:05.172 --> 00:50:13.038
I'm going to see what's wrong with him, if he dead or what."

779
00:50:13.038 --> 00:50:20.299
And he said you could couldn't even get close to the house.

780
00:50:20.299 --> 00:50:23.325
It was smelling so much.

781
00:50:23.325 --> 00:50:25.140
He was ninety-seven.

782
00:50:25.140 --> 00:50:36.032
And he had neighbors close to him that, I know they smell him, but they never say anything.

783
00:50:36.032 --> 00:50:39.057
Yeah, that's how he died.

784
00:50:39.057 --> 00:50:43.293
The funeral home said they couldn’t tell.

785
00:50:43.293 --> 00:50:48.134
He was too decayed for them to tell.

786
00:50:48.134 --> 00:50:50.554
And that was sad.

787
00:50:50.554 --> 00:50:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember how he made his whiskey?

788
00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:56.441
<v WASHINGTON>Hmm?

789
00:50:56.441 --> 00:50:59.966
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember how he made his whiskey?

790
00:50:59.966 --> 00:51:01.729
<v WASHINGTON>I know some corn.

791
00:51:01.729 --> 00:51:03.051
Boil that corn.

792
00:51:03.051 --> 00:51:07.458
That's all I see him put in that is corn.

793
00:51:07.458 --> 00:51:10.102
And did it so many days.

794
00:51:10.102 --> 00:51:14.068
I don't know what he put in it though.

795
00:51:14.068 --> 00:51:15.390
I don't know.

796
00:51:15.390 --> 00:51:17.153
<v ARSENEAULT>And he sold it?

797
00:51:17.153 --> 00:51:17.593
<v WASHINGTON>Yes.

798
00:51:17.593 --> 00:51:18.475
<v ARSENEAULT>How much?

799
00:51:18.475 --> 00:51:22.000
Do you remember how much it was?

800
00:51:22.000 --> 00:51:25.684
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] Yes, he would sell it.

801
00:51:25.684 --> 00:51:28.140
He made the tea.

802
00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:31.825
He wouldn't charge for the tea.

803
00:51:31.825 --> 00:51:34.895
Just make it for them.

804
00:51:34.895 --> 00:51:43.491
And I remember old lady that she was really sick and she got better.

805
00:51:43.491 --> 00:51:52.088
I don't know what it was, but I know that something from the woods.

806
00:51:52.088 --> 00:51:58.228
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he have any other remedies that he would use?

807
00:51:58.228 --> 00:52:01.298
<v WASHINGTON>[Disagrees] That's all I know.

808
00:52:01.298 --> 00:52:07.439
If he treated them some other ways he didn't tell.

809
00:52:07.439 --> 00:52:13.579
But he was not a man to talk that much.

810
00:52:13.579 --> 00:52:18.491
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he learn to be a treater?

811
00:52:18.491 --> 00:52:20.333
<v WASHINGTON>I don't know.

812
00:52:20.333 --> 00:52:23.404
He would go to church.

813
00:52:23.404 --> 00:52:29.544
He had the horses, and he had a race horse.

814
00:52:29.544 --> 00:52:38.754
Him and my uncle, my mama’s brother, they would bring their horse to Jennings [Louisiana].

815
00:52:38.754 --> 00:52:42.439
And they had race horses tracks.

816
00:52:42.439 --> 00:52:52.263
And his horse won first place two times in the parade in Lafayette and in Jennings.

817
00:52:52.263 --> 00:52:55.333
But he liked the horses.

818
00:52:55.333 --> 00:53:02.088
And his picture on the wall over there, with the horse.

819
00:53:02.088 --> 00:53:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you help him train the horses?

820
00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:10.077
<v WASHINGTON>[Disagrees] He do it himself.

821
00:53:10.077 --> 00:53:18.692
He and my uncle, my mama’s brother, they was always together like two brothers.

822
00:53:18.692 --> 00:53:21.769
And they was [brought up].

823
00:53:21.769 --> 00:53:23.615
The other one

824
00:53:23.615 --> 00:53:27.923
<c.p>[Jewel?] had some horses too . . . race horses.

825
00:53:27.923 --> 00:53:32.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what kind they were?

826
00:53:32.231 --> 00:53:34.692
What kind of horses?

827
00:53:34.692 --> 00:53:36.538
<v WASHINGTON>I don't know.

828
00:53:36.538 --> 00:53:37.769
I guess . . .

829
00:53:37.769 --> 00:53:38.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

830
00:53:38.385 --> 00:53:39.615
Just curious.

831
00:53:39.615 --> 00:53:44.538
If it was a special breed or something.

832
00:53:44.538 --> 00:53:50.077
<v WASHINGTON>This was the boy horse, this one right there.

833
00:53:50.077 --> 00:53:51.923
<v ARSENEAULT>The boy horse?

834
00:53:51.923 --> 00:53:55.615
<v WASHINGTON>But we had a good life.

835
00:53:55.615 --> 00:54:03.615
I can't talk about nobody mistreating me, but refused me to sit down.

836
00:54:03.615 --> 00:54:06.692
That's all I can remember.

837
00:54:06.692 --> 00:54:13.462
But when I came to Westlake I was friendly with everybody.

838
00:54:13.462 --> 00:54:14.077
Everybody.

839
00:54:14.077 --> 00:54:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did you come to Westlake?

840
00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:19.867
<v WASHINGTON>Forty-something.

841
00:54:19.867 --> 00:54:20.733
<v ARSENEAULT>‘43?

842
00:54:20.733 --> 00:54:21.600
<v WASHINGTON>‘42.

843
00:54:21.600 --> 00:54:27.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember anything about the protests?

844
00:54:27.667 --> 00:54:29.400
<v WASHINGTON>About who?

845
00:54:29.400 --> 00:54:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The protests.

846
00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:33.030
<v WASHINGTON>Oh yes.

847
00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:35.089
I saw that, yeah.

848
00:54:35.089 --> 00:54:37.664
But it didn't bother me.

849
00:54:37.664 --> 00:54:39.724
Nobody didn't mistreat me.

850
00:54:39.724 --> 00:54:44.358
You say I'm lying, but that's really the truth.

851
00:54:44.358 --> 00:54:48.992
I don't know what part is that bother me.

852
00:54:48.992 --> 00:54:52.596
The little children were crazy about me.

853
00:54:52.596 --> 00:54:53.626
Right here.

854
00:54:53.626 --> 00:54:54.656
My neighbors.

855
00:54:54.656 --> 00:54:58.775
I say one day he came . . . Big man.

856
00:54:58.775 --> 00:55:02.894
He say, "I want to ask you something."

857
00:55:02.894 --> 00:55:03.924
Said, "What?"

858
00:55:03.924 --> 00:55:07.528
He said, "What it is about you?

859
00:55:07.528 --> 00:55:10.103
You different from other people."

860
00:55:10.103 --> 00:55:12.163
I said, "What way?"

861
00:55:12.163 --> 00:55:16.797
He said, "You treat us like I'm your child."

862
00:55:16.797 --> 00:55:20.401
He said, "One night we was bored.

863
00:55:20.401 --> 00:55:21.946
Children start fighting."

864
00:55:21.946 --> 00:55:25.035
You know how they act often.

865
00:55:25.035 --> 00:55:27.610
They play ball right there.

866
00:55:27.610 --> 00:55:32.244
I'd go outside and say, "Which one started first?"

867
00:55:32.244 --> 00:55:33.789
They'd tell me.

868
00:55:33.789 --> 00:55:36.878
I'd say, "Well babe, go home.

869
00:55:36.878 --> 00:55:39.453
Go by your mama there."

870
00:55:39.453 --> 00:55:44.087
Whether he didn't want to go I'd say, "Go.

871
00:55:44.087 --> 00:55:45.117
Go home."

872
00:55:45.117 --> 00:55:46.146
Play together.

873
00:55:46.146 --> 00:55:49.751
They can play if they don't fight.

874
00:55:49.751 --> 00:55:53.355
The one fight, go meet your mama.

875
00:55:53.355 --> 00:55:54.385
And that' [ . . . ?].

876
00:55:54.385 --> 00:56:02.108
And he say, "I can ask you if you mind if I call you mama?"

877
00:56:02.108 --> 00:56:06.743
I said, "Why you want to call me mama?"

878
00:56:06.743 --> 00:56:18.585
He said, "When I was little you sent me home when I start fighting, and you make me the man I is today."

879
00:56:18.585 --> 00:56:20.645
I said, "Well really?

880
00:56:20.645 --> 00:56:25.279
I don't even remember you when you was little."

881
00:56:25.279 --> 00:56:27.339
He said, "I remember."

882
00:56:27.339 --> 00:56:34.547
And if you go somewhere, he want to take me with his mama too.

883
00:56:34.547 --> 00:56:47.935
And he said, "My mama was home, but she did not say ‘stop fighting or go in the yard and play in the yard.'" He said,

884
00:56:47.935 --> 00:56:52.569
"My mama stay in the house, and you're outside.

885
00:56:52.569 --> 00:56:55.144
You hear what’s going on."

886
00:56:55.144 --> 00:57:01.837
And my nephew was staying right next door then, the oldest one boy.

887
00:57:01.837 --> 00:57:03.897
When I told him,

888
00:57:03.897 --> 00:57:12.136
<c.p>“Go home,” he took the ball and he pass it through the window in the room.

889
00:57:12.136 --> 00:57:13.680
He throw it.

890
00:57:13.680 --> 00:57:15.225
Bust the window.

891
00:57:15.225 --> 00:57:18.829
I didn't report him to his daddy.

892
00:57:18.829 --> 00:57:20.889
I didn't say nothing.

893
00:57:20.889 --> 00:57:30.672
And I said, "Boy, you don't want to listen but somebody going to train you on the rules somewhere."

894
00:57:30.672 --> 00:57:32.217
He dead now.

895
00:57:32.217 --> 00:57:35.821
Yes, he was the best basketball player.

896
00:57:35.821 --> 00:57:37.366
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>In Lake Charles.

897
00:57:37.366 --> 00:57:39.425
He went to Mossville.

898
00:57:39.425 --> 00:57:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You remember his name?

899
00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:42.527
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Johnathon [ . . .?].

900
00:57:42.527 --> 00:57:44.108
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, got you.

901
00:57:44.108 --> 00:57:45.162
Johnny Comeaux.

902
00:57:45.162 --> 00:57:46.216
<v WASHINGTON>My nephew.

903
00:57:46.216 --> 00:57:50.432
<v ARSENEAULT>I read about him in the old papers.

904
00:57:50.432 --> 00:57:58.865
<v WASHINGTON>I didn't tell his mother I felt sorry for her, because I said, "You play ball.

905
00:57:58.865 --> 00:58:00.973
You act good maybe.

906
00:58:00.973 --> 00:58:02.554
I don't know."

907
00:58:02.554 --> 00:58:05.716
He was a tough little boy.

908
00:58:05.716 --> 00:58:09.405
And he played basketball for Grambling [University].

909
00:58:09.405 --> 00:58:10.459
Number one.

910
00:58:10.459 --> 00:58:15.730
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever see any of his games at Grambling?

911
00:58:15.730 --> 00:58:21.000
Did you ever go to any of his games?

912
00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:23.561
<v WASHINGTON>Did I ever went to the game?

913
00:58:23.561 --> 00:58:23.927
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees]

914
00:58:23.927 --> 00:58:25.390
<v WASHINGTON>I wouldn't go nowhere.

915
00:58:25.390 --> 00:58:28.317
I went and take care of my children.

916
00:58:28.317 --> 00:58:29.780
I wouldn't visit them.

917
00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:37.829
They was . . . I'd say you see me and I'll sit on the swing and I enjoyed sitting being outside on the swing.

918
00:58:37.829 --> 00:58:41.488
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you go to any of the games in Mossville?

919
00:58:41.488 --> 00:58:41.854
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

920
00:58:41.854 --> 00:58:42.951
My boy played.

921
00:58:42.951 --> 00:58:44.780
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you see Johnny play?

922
00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:45.878
<v WASHINGTON>My boy played.

923
00:58:45.878 --> 00:58:46.976
<v ARSENEUALT>Your sons played?

924
00:58:46.976 --> 00:58:47.341
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

925
00:58:47.341 --> 00:58:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they play on the same team with Johnny?

926
00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:51.475
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

927
00:58:51.475 --> 00:58:51.950
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

928
00:58:51.950 --> 00:58:55.752
<v WASHINGTON>Because they was . . . My brother was right there.

929
00:58:55.752 --> 00:58:59.079
Then the next house, my other brother.

930
00:58:59.079 --> 00:59:00.030
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

931
00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:03.832
<v WASHINGTON>He just had one boy but four girls.

932
00:59:03.832 --> 00:59:07.158
And they keep in touch with me.

933
00:59:07.158 --> 00:59:11.436
They thank me for what I did for them.

934
00:59:11.436 --> 00:59:16.188
<v ARSENEAULT>I think that was a good team those couple years.

935
00:59:16.188 --> 00:59:20.465
They got a lot of reviews in the paper.

936
00:59:20.465 --> 00:59:27.594
I saw it was Johnny Comeaux and I think McKeever Edwards were on the team.

937
00:59:27.594 --> 00:59:36.149
And then the Payne brothers were all really big men and they were all in the papers together.

938
00:59:36.149 --> 00:59:36.624
Yeah.

939
00:59:36.624 --> 00:59:37.574
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>[Lionell Goodwin?]

940
00:59:37.574 --> 00:59:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That's right.

941
00:59:39.000 --> 00:59:39.574
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

942
00:59:39.574 --> 00:59:41.871
Had a good time.

943
00:59:41.871 --> 00:59:44.742
I had a good time.

944
00:59:44.742 --> 00:59:47.612
I had a good life.

945
00:59:47.612 --> 00:59:49.909
I went to church.

946
00:59:49.909 --> 00:59:52.780
I worked for the church.

947
00:59:52.780 --> 00:59:53.354
Did [ . . . ?].

948
00:59:53.354 --> 01:00:03.689
So say I was . . . Decided they want to build a church and I said, "I'll pass and bake."

949
01:00:03.689 --> 01:00:04.837
"You will?"

950
01:00:04.837 --> 01:00:06.560
I say, "Yeah.

951
01:00:06.560 --> 01:00:12.301
I'll pass and bake to all the [soldiers?] in Westlake."

952
01:00:12.301 --> 01:00:17.469
And one man say, "Lady, how old are you?"

953
01:00:17.469 --> 01:00:19.191
I said, "Fifty."

954
01:00:19.191 --> 01:00:19.766
"Fifty,

955
01:00:19.766 --> 01:00:23.785
<c.p>and you there baking for the church?"

956
01:00:23.785 --> 01:00:25.507
I said, "Yes."

957
01:00:25.507 --> 01:00:31.249
We a group of ladies and we want a church.

958
01:00:31.249 --> 01:00:37.565
It's like . . . So we did build a church, Saint John Bosco.

959
01:00:37.565 --> 01:00:38.713
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

960
01:00:38.713 --> 01:00:47.325
<v WASHINGTON>And they named it . . . I'm the mother of that church because I did the most.

961
01:00:47.325 --> 01:00:48.474
It's alright.

962
01:00:48.474 --> 01:00:51.344
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>It was segregated back then.

963
01:00:51.344 --> 01:00:51.919
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah.

964
01:00:51.919 --> 01:00:59.383
We had a side of the black and a side for the white.

965
01:00:59.383 --> 01:01:02.828
But we never had no trouble.

966
01:01:02.828 --> 01:01:05.124
Nobody didn't [harm?] nobody.

967
01:01:05.124 --> 01:01:14.885
So not too long ago there they called me in and say, "We want to treat you."

968
01:01:14.885 --> 01:01:20.053
So I went to the city, to the park.

969
01:01:20.053 --> 01:01:22.923
It come on the paper.

970
01:01:22.923 --> 01:01:27.517
I don't know if I still got it.

971
01:01:27.517 --> 01:01:29.239
Y'all got it?

972
01:01:29.239 --> 01:01:36.129
At the park when you had to go and they honor me.

973
01:01:36.129 --> 01:01:39.000
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>Our book is basically . . .

974
01:01:39.000 --> 01:01:42.693
<v ARSENEAULT>I want to look at that after.

975
01:01:42.693 --> 01:01:44.803
<v WASHINGTON>But I enjoyed it.

976
01:01:44.803 --> 01:01:46.386
I enjoyed it.

977
01:01:46.386 --> 01:01:49.551
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about school?

978
01:01:49.551 --> 01:01:53.772
<v WASHINGTON>I didn't have no trouble either at school.

979
01:01:53.772 --> 01:01:58.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you learn English at school or at home?

980
01:01:58.520 --> 01:02:07.488
<v WASHINGTON>No, I went . . . When I was young our cousin [?] Arceneaux, he give the land for black school.

981
01:02:07.488 --> 01:02:11.709
But they didn't name it the black school.

982
01:02:11.709 --> 01:02:15.929
They say, for black kids and Indian kids.

983
01:02:15.929 --> 01:02:19.622
And I went up to eighth grade.

984
01:02:19.622 --> 01:02:25.953
And then when I came to Westlake they had school at night.

985
01:02:25.953 --> 01:02:28.591
So I went to that.

986
01:02:28.591 --> 01:02:33.339
So they give me up until twelfth grade education.

987
01:02:33.339 --> 01:02:36.504
But I did my business myself.

988
01:02:36.504 --> 01:02:39.142
I didn't have no problem.

989
01:02:39.142 --> 01:02:42.835
<v ARSENEUALT>I'm curious about your school in Carencro.

990
01:02:42.835 --> 01:02:46.000
What did it look like?

991
01:02:46.000 --> 01:02:48.212
<v WASHINGTON>It was nice.

992
01:02:48.212 --> 01:03:00.012
Yes, they both could . . . They didn't break it, they hauled it to Carencro in the city.

993
01:03:00.012 --> 01:03:02.962
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it a one-room?

994
01:03:02.962 --> 01:03:08.125
<v WASHINGTON>No, they had a room for boys . . .

995
01:03:08.125 --> 01:03:08.862
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

996
01:03:08.862 --> 01:03:12.550
<v WASHINGTON>. . . and a room for girls.

997
01:03:12.550 --> 01:03:17.712
They had two teachers, was from Lafayette.

998
01:03:17.712 --> 01:03:19.188
<v ARSENEAULT>Two teachers?

999
01:03:19.188 --> 01:03:23.612
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] Ms. Agnes Bright was one.

1000
01:03:23.612 --> 01:03:29.512
The other girl was a Mouton, Barbara Mouton.

1001
01:03:29.512 --> 01:03:32.462
They were good teachers.

1002
01:03:32.462 --> 01:03:35.412
Didn't have no problem.

1003
01:03:35.412 --> 01:03:39.100
And it was [right?] [ . . . ?] Arceneaux.

1004
01:03:39.100 --> 01:03:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How far was it from your house?

1005
01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:51.183
<v WASHINGTON>From my house we just had to cross the road.

1006
01:03:51.183 --> 01:03:51.801
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

1007
01:03:51.801 --> 01:03:53.656
I wasn't far?

1008
01:03:53.656 --> 01:03:54.274
Oh.

1009
01:03:54.274 --> 01:03:56.129
<v WASHINGTON>Right at home.

1010
01:03:56.129 --> 01:04:00.456
We’d go eat home for dinner . . . lunch.

1011
01:04:00.456 --> 01:04:03.548
Then go back to school.

1012
01:04:03.548 --> 01:04:07.257
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite subject?

1013
01:04:07.257 --> 01:04:09.730
<v WASHINGTON>I liked to spell.

1014
01:04:09.730 --> 01:04:12.203
We had spelling match.

1015
01:04:12.203 --> 01:04:16.531
I was the number one for spelling.

1016
01:04:16.531 --> 01:04:19.004
I learned to spell.

1017
01:04:19.004 --> 01:04:25.187
And the children got so much trouble now to learn.

1018
01:04:25.187 --> 01:04:29.515
I don't know what the reason is.

1019
01:04:29.515 --> 01:04:31.988
It's a different school.

1020
01:04:31.988 --> 01:04:36.315
You know your ABC's and your spelling.

1021
01:04:36.315 --> 01:04:38.788
That was just good.

1022
01:04:38.788 --> 01:04:46.207
And I went . . . My sister had her little child . . . grandchild that died.

1023
01:04:46.207 --> 01:04:56.100
And I went to the funeral in Lafayette right by Mouton [Switch?] as you enter Lafayette.

1024
01:04:56.100 --> 01:05:01.664
And the most of the people there we [DeQuire?].

1025
01:05:01.664 --> 01:05:10.938
Their last name was [DeQuire?], and I wonder, but we didn't stay after the funeral.

1026
01:05:10.938 --> 01:05:26.394
<c.p>My son-in-law took us [. . .?] grandson that drowned in the . . . They had a little swimming pool outside and she left them with the big little girl.

1027
01:05:26.394 --> 01:05:33.195
And I don't know what happened but the little baby drowned.

1028
01:05:33.195 --> 01:05:35.050
Little boy drowned.

1029
01:05:35.050 --> 01:05:38.759
And we went to that funeral.

1030
01:05:38.759 --> 01:05:47.415
I ain't never see so many [DeQuire?] in the area as you enter Lafayette.

1031
01:05:47.415 --> 01:05:55.452
So I didn't talk with nobody after the funeral so I would ask.

1032
01:05:55.452 --> 01:06:00.398
It may be the same teacher was there.

1033
01:06:00.398 --> 01:06:06.581
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>She would have had to have been over a hundred.

1034
01:06:06.581 --> 01:06:07.199
<v WASHINGTON>Hmm?

1035
01:06:07.199 --> 01:06:14.000
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>She would have had to have been over a hundred.

1036
01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:15.294
<v WASHINGTON>Oh yeah.

1037
01:06:15.294 --> 01:06:19.824
Yeah, because she was a young teacher.

1038
01:06:19.824 --> 01:06:25.647
Just graduated from Lafayette. [Agnes DeQuire?] and Barbara Mouton.

1039
01:06:25.647 --> 01:06:30.824
<v ARSENEAULT>Were all the same grades in one room?

1040
01:06:30.824 --> 01:06:31.471
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees]

1041
01:06:31.471 --> 01:06:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they separate the work?

1042
01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:40.822
<v WASHINGTON>We just had classes in different spots.

1043
01:06:40.822 --> 01:06:43.578
Ms. DeQuire had hers.

1044
01:06:43.578 --> 01:06:46.333
Ms. Mouton had hers.

1045
01:06:46.333 --> 01:06:49.778
And they was young ladies.

1046
01:06:49.778 --> 01:06:53.911
Young, wonderful ladies. [They stayed at

1047
01:06:53.911 --> 01:06:55.289
<c.p>Sister Arceneaux?].

1048
01:06:55.289 --> 01:06:58.733
<v ARSENEAULT>They stayed at the school?

1049
01:06:58.733 --> 01:07:02.178
They slept there any everything?

1050
01:07:02.178 --> 01:07:06.311
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] And they would visit us.

1051
01:07:06.311 --> 01:07:09.756
They would play with us.

1052
01:07:09.756 --> 01:07:16.644
I was younger than them, but they play with us.

1053
01:07:16.644 --> 01:07:16.844
My oldest sister was they age.

1054
01:07:16.644 --> 01:07:20.778
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>[ . . . ?]

1055
01:07:20.778 --> 01:07:22.156
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1056
01:07:22.156 --> 01:07:29.733
So do you remember using the telephone for the first time?

1057
01:07:29.733 --> 01:07:30.422
<v WASHINGTON>Hmm?

1058
01:07:30.422 --> 01:07:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember using a phone for the first time?

1059
01:07:38.000 --> 01:07:38.631
<v WASHINGTON>Yes.

1060
01:07:38.631 --> 01:07:41.787
I remember using the phone.

1061
01:07:41.787 --> 01:07:44.943
We didn't have no phone.

1062
01:07:44.943 --> 01:07:51.885
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you use one . . . the first time you used one?

1063
01:07:51.885 --> 01:07:55.041
<v WASHINGTON>When I got married here.

1064
01:07:55.041 --> 01:07:56.303
<v ARSENEAULT>In Westlake?

1065
01:07:56.303 --> 01:07:59.459
<v WASHINGTON>[Agrees] Yeah, we were neighbors.

1066
01:07:59.459 --> 01:08:01.352
We would visit.

1067
01:08:01.352 --> 01:08:04.508
We didn't have no telephones.

1068
01:08:04.508 --> 01:08:08.295
I didn't grow up with phones.

1069
01:08:08.295 --> 01:08:13.344
The only thing we had was a radio.

1070
01:08:13.344 --> 01:08:18.393
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you listen to on the radio?

1071
01:08:18.393 --> 01:08:20.287
<v WASHINGTON>And no fan.

1072
01:08:20.287 --> 01:08:23.443
We didn't have no fan.

1073
01:08:23.443 --> 01:08:28.492
We had heat with wood fireplace, fire stove.

1074
01:08:28.492 --> 01:08:44.902
When I got married, when I move in my first little apartment where I was staying I had a heater, and then we had a fan.

1075
01:08:44.902 --> 01:08:46.164
That's it.

1076
01:08:46.164 --> 01:08:48.057
Didn't have light.

1077
01:08:48.057 --> 01:08:49.951
We had lamp.

1078
01:08:49.951 --> 01:08:53.107
I still got my lamp.

1079
01:08:53.107 --> 01:08:53.738
<v ARSENEAULT>Kerosene?

1080
01:08:53.738 --> 01:08:55.000
Yeah.

1081
01:08:55.000 --> 01:08:59.280
<v WASHINGTON>Yeah, but to me that was a good life.

1082
01:08:59.280 --> 01:09:01.658
I had a good life.

1083
01:09:01.658 --> 01:09:02.133
Yeah.

1084
01:09:02.133 --> 01:09:08.316
The doctor when I told him he said, "I want to question you."

1085
01:09:08.316 --> 01:09:09.267
Said, "What?

1086
01:09:09.267 --> 01:09:11.169
Question me for what?"

1087
01:09:11.169 --> 01:09:12.596
"How you live."

1088
01:09:12.596 --> 01:09:15.924
I said, "I had a good life."

1089
01:09:15.924 --> 01:09:22.107
And he said, "You don't mind me asking you, did you ever drink?"

1090
01:09:22.107 --> 01:09:24.960
I said, "No, I never drink."

1091
01:09:24.960 --> 01:09:28.289
I didn't tell him my bootleg whiskey.

1092
01:09:28.289 --> 01:09:29.240
My daddy.

1093
01:09:29.240 --> 01:09:31.618
I said, "I never drunk."

1094
01:09:31.618 --> 01:09:36.849
One more, after I'm married one of my cousins got married.

1095
01:09:36.849 --> 01:09:45.409
And they had a big reception, and I know my two brothers that they would drink, you know.

1096
01:09:45.409 --> 01:09:48.738
The one right there would drink more.

1097
01:09:48.738 --> 01:09:51.116
But he raised his family.

1098
01:09:51.116 --> 01:09:52.067
Did good.

1099
01:09:52.067 --> 01:09:59.200
And they had a lot of bottles of liquor on the top of the table.

1100
01:09:59.200 --> 01:10:08.711
And they went danced I took two of them big bottles and put them under the table at that place.

1101
01:10:08.711 --> 01:10:11.089
And I never told nobody.

1102
01:10:11.089 --> 01:10:16.320
I don't know if they see it was missing, you know,

1103
01:10:16.320 --> 01:10:25.831
but keep them . . . And when I told them that after everything was closed I say, "Y'all quit dancing the Carmouche[?]."

1104
01:10:25.831 --> 01:10:32.489
They had the club in Lafayette on Highway 90 going to Carencro and Lafayette.

1105
01:10:32.489 --> 01:10:34.391
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

1106
01:10:34.391 --> 01:10:35.342
<v WASHINGTON>The [Carmouche?].

1107
01:10:35.342 --> 01:10:36.769
<v ARSENEAULT>The [Carmouche?], okay.

1108
01:10:36.769 --> 01:10:39.147
Is that somebody's last name?

1109
01:10:39.147 --> 01:10:41.049
<v WASHINGTON>That's their last name.

1110
01:10:41.049 --> 01:10:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1111
01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:45.760
<v WASHINGTON>They had a club right off of 90.

1112
01:10:45.760 --> 01:10:47.169
They always dance.

1113
01:10:47.169 --> 01:10:55.628
I didn't go dance, and I took them off the table and I’d hide them under the table.

1114
01:10:55.628 --> 01:10:59.858
And I don't know if they ever find them.

1115
01:10:59.858 --> 01:11:03.148
I guess I felt everything was [clear?].

1116
01:11:03.148 --> 01:11:05.027
We had to leave.

1117
01:11:05.027 --> 01:11:08.317
We had to come back to Westlake.

1118
01:11:08.317 --> 01:11:11.607
And I told that to my husband.

1119
01:11:11.607 --> 01:11:19.596
I said, "I took two bottle off of that table and I put it under the table."

1120
01:11:19.596 --> 01:11:23.825
And I said, "I wanted them to find them."

1121
01:11:23.825 --> 01:11:26.175
Yeah, they missed them two.

1122
01:11:26.175 --> 01:11:30.874
I said, "They were all drunk before they go home."

1123
01:11:30.874 --> 01:11:33.694
And that was all my cousins.

1124
01:11:33.694 --> 01:11:36.514
They cursed me out for that.

1125
01:11:36.514 --> 01:11:39.803
I said, "That's not . . . Y'all didn't drink."

1126
01:11:39.803 --> 01:11:41.683
I never did drink.

1127
01:11:41.683 --> 01:11:43.563
I never did smoke.

1128
01:11:43.563 --> 01:11:44.973
I wouldn't try.

1129
01:11:44.973 --> 01:11:48.262
Yeah, and doctor said, "Well that's good.

1130
01:11:48.262 --> 01:11:53.432
I don't know how you did it, but you did it."

1131
01:11:53.432 --> 01:11:56.251
I said, "Yes, I did it.

1132
01:11:56.251 --> 01:12:00.011
You do a thing if you want to.

1133
01:12:00.011 --> 01:12:03.301
You want to drink, you go drink."

1134
01:12:03.301 --> 01:12:11.879
But oh no . . . on the ninety, oh man, no. I didn't say my children didn't drink.

1135
01:12:11.879 --> 01:12:20.190
My oldest son, he never was a drinker much, but he went in the service.

1136
01:12:20.190 --> 01:12:22.407
He was a marine.

1137
01:12:22.407 --> 01:12:26.285
I don't know what happened to him.

1138
01:12:26.285 --> 01:12:33.489
He don't talk about it if he don't want to talk about it.

1139
01:12:33.489 --> 01:12:37.921
But he really . . . The Vietnam . . . The whatever it

1140
01:12:37.921 --> 01:12:44.016
<c.p>was . . . And my baby son was . . . he volunteered for the service.

1141
01:12:44.016 --> 01:12:47.341
They both went to the service.

1142
01:12:47.341 --> 01:12:56.761
They draft my oldest son, and they say all his group got killed and he didn't die.

1143
01:12:56.761 --> 01:13:02.302
But he went mentally, and they discharged him from mentally.

1144
01:13:02.302 --> 01:13:05.626
And he still off and on.

1145
01:13:05.626 --> 01:13:08.951
He don't want to go nowhere.

1146
01:13:08.951 --> 01:13:15.046
But he got his own home, and he talk about it.

1147
01:13:15.046 --> 01:13:20.587
He don't want to . . . He [ . . . ?] nobody too much about it.

1148
01:13:20.587 --> 01:13:25.020
And then my baby son, he got killed.

1149
01:13:25.020 --> 01:13:27.790
They say he killed himself.

1150
01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:36.102
And I don't believe this, because he had wanted to be the mayor for Westlake.

1151
01:13:36.102 --> 01:13:44.967
He was to take his office that Monday, but he came to me that Saturday afternoon.

1152
01:13:44.967 --> 01:13:53.279
And he said, "Mom, I want you to come see how I fixed my office."

1153
01:13:53.279 --> 01:14:02.698
I said, "I'll go with you Monday, and I’ll stay with you while you're over there Monday.”

1154
01:14:02.698 --> 01:14:07.685
And the people come and he didn't say nothing.

1155
01:14:07.685 --> 01:14:11.010
And he just kept on talking.

1156
01:14:11.010 --> 01:14:17.659
"I would like for you to go see how I did it."

1157
01:14:17.659 --> 01:14:25.416
And I see it and that was Saturday afternoon late, but not that dark.

1158
01:14:25.416 --> 01:14:27.633
And the phone ring.

1159
01:14:27.633 --> 01:14:31.511
He had the phone in his pocket.

1160
01:14:31.511 --> 01:14:35.390
Phone rings, and he answer the phone.

1161
01:14:35.390 --> 01:14:48.134
But I don't know what they told him, but I know they talked to him about the three seven [Tressette, a card game].

1162
01:14:48.134 --> 01:14:55.338
I know he elected to go to the boat . . . go to the boat.

1163
01:14:55.338 --> 01:14:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The casino?

1164
01:14:57.000 --> 01:15:04.412
<v WASHINGTON>But he didn't want them to ask him if he win or if he lose.

1165
01:15:04.412 --> 01:15:04.906
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1166
01:15:04.906 --> 01:15:07.871
<v WASHINGTON>He said, "It make me lose . . .

1167
01:15:07.871 --> 01:15:08.859
<v ARSENEAULT>Bad luck.

1168
01:15:08.859 --> 01:15:10.835
<v WASHINGTON>. . . if I tell you.”

1169
01:15:10.835 --> 01:15:15.776
And somebody called him and say something the three seven.

1170
01:15:15.776 --> 01:15:19.235
And he said, "I know you joking."

1171
01:15:19.235 --> 01:15:21.212
Whoever he talk with.

1172
01:15:21.212 --> 01:15:29.118
I couldn't hear what the other fellow said or a lady or whatever . . . who it was.

1173
01:15:29.118 --> 01:15:33.071
And I didn't want to ask after that.

1174
01:15:33.071 --> 01:15:36.035
But he say, "The three seven."

1175
01:15:36.035 --> 01:15:37.518
Then he left.

1176
01:15:37.518 --> 01:15:39.988
And they say something else.

1177
01:15:39.988 --> 01:15:49.376
He said, "But I can't meet y'all now because I'm at my mama's, and I walked to my mama's.

1178
01:15:49.376 --> 01:15:51.847
I left my truck home.

1179
01:15:51.847 --> 01:15:55.306
I have to go get it first."

1180
01:15:55.306 --> 01:16:00.741
I hear him say that, and they say something else too.

1181
01:16:00.741 --> 01:16:04.200
And he said, "I'll meet y'all later.

1182
01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:05.682
I'll meet y'all."

1183
01:16:05.682 --> 01:16:09.141
But he said, "I know you joking."

1184
01:16:09.141 --> 01:16:11.612
And that's how he left.

1185
01:16:11.612 --> 01:16:15.071
And that was the end of him.

1186
01:16:15.071 --> 01:16:16.059
Got killed.

1187
01:16:16.059 --> 01:16:17.047
Got shot.

1188
01:16:17.047 --> 01:16:18.035
One bullet.

1189
01:16:18.035 --> 01:16:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did they find him?

1190
01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:22.500
<v WASHINGTON>On Mossville property.

1191
01:16:22.500 --> 01:16:25.000
Where they had the chair.

1192
01:16:25.000 --> 01:16:28.500
People would stop and see it there.

1193
01:16:28.500 --> 01:16:32.000
They had a band . . . a table too.

1194
01:16:32.000 --> 01:16:36.000
And they say that's where he got killed.

1195
01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:40.000
